-- ===========================================================================
-- 0001_delivery_failed_status — `orders_status_check` learns 'delivery_failed'.
--
-- Generated by drizzle-kit from packages/db/src/schema/shop.ts (the widened
-- `orderStatuses` array), then annotated by hand: the comments below are the
-- only edits, exactly as in 0000_init. This file, not the TypeScript, is what a
-- reviewer who does not know Drizzle reads.
--
-- WHAT THIS BUYS, AND WHY IT SHIPS BEFORE THE CODE THAT NEEDS IT
--
-- `delivery_failed` is the shop's statement about itself — "we did not hand
-- over a key". Phases 1 and 2 could not reach it, so it was deliberately not a
-- value the database would accept (0000_init says so at the constraint). Phase 3
-- adds the code that reaches it, and the CHECK is the tripwire standing between
-- the two: with the code deployed and this migration not applied, the FIRST
-- order the shop tries to record as failed takes a
--
--     23514 check_violation  "orders_status_check"
--
-- inside the transaction that was recording the failure. That transaction rolls
-- back, the order rests in `delivering`, and the migration meant to make stuck
-- orders visible has instead created one. Hence: migrations precede the deploy
-- (`pnpm db:migrate`, the natural order), and this file ships in the same commit
-- as the widened schema (spec 003 technical-considerations §10 and §11 R6).
--
-- WHY `text` + CHECK AND NOT A POSTGRES ENUM — THIS IS THE MIGRATION THAT
-- DECISION WAS MADE FOR
--
-- Widening a CHECK is ordinary DDL: it runs inside the migration transaction
-- and the new value is usable by the very next statement in the same file.
-- `ALTER TYPE ... ADD VALUE` cannot use the label it just added in the
-- transaction that added it, so an enum would forbid widening and then writing
-- the new value in one migration — and would forbid, for instance, a backfill
-- sitting under the widening. The column stays `text`; the CHECK stays a CHECK.
-- Weakening it to a bare `text` column would make every ordering question in
-- this phase easy and delete the tripwire above. Do not.
--
-- MEASURED (Postgres 16.11, this project's container, 20 000-order fixture,
-- inside a rolled-back transaction — spec 003 technical-considerations §10):
--
--     widen orders_status_check ....... 3.6 ms
--     lock taken ...................... ACCESS EXCLUSIVE
--     table rewrite ................... NONE (one full scan to validate)
--
-- The `NOT VALID` / `VALIDATE CONSTRAINT` two-step was measured too — 1.5 ms
-- plus 3.0 ms — and DELIBERATELY NOT ADOPTED. It exists to keep a long
-- validating scan off the write path; 3.6 ms on the largest table this shop will
-- ever have does not buy two statements and a window in which the constraint is
-- present but unenforced. Considered, rejected, recorded here so the next reader
-- does not have to re-derive it.
--
-- ATOMICITY — verified, not assumed. `packages/db/src/migrate.ts` calls Drizzle's
-- migrator, whose pg dialect wraps every pending file AND its
-- `drizzle.__drizzle_migrations` row in one `session.transaction(...)`. The DROP
-- and the ADD below are therefore two statements inside one transaction holding
-- ACCESS EXCLUSIVE on `orders`: there is no committed instant in which `orders`
-- has no status CHECK, and a failure half-way leaves neither the DDL nor the
-- journal entry.
--
-- ORDERING, WITHIN THIS FILE AND AGAINST WHAT COMES NEXT. Nothing in this file
-- depends on anything else in it. Two later pieces depend on THIS one:
--
--   * `orders_undelivered_idx` (slice 4) is a partial index whose predicate
--     names 'delivery_failed'. It cannot be created before this CHECK admits
--     that value — the predicate would name a status the constraint forbids.
--   * The `markDeliveryFailed` transition (slice 1) writes the value at runtime.
--
-- Scope: this file is the CHECK and nothing else. `issuance_attempts.attempt` /
-- `probe_count`, `UNIQUE (order_id, attempt)`, `supplier_requests.provider` and
-- the phase's indexes belong to later slices and to later files, so each slice
-- stays independently runnable.
-- ===========================================================================

-- Postgres has no "widen a CHECK" verb, so the pair below IS the widening:
-- drop the six-value constraint and immediately re-add the seven-value one.
-- Both statements are in the one transaction described above.
--
--   -- before: CHECK (status IN ('created','paid','delivering','delivered',
--   --                           'payment_failed','out_of_stock'))
--   -- after:  the same, plus 'delivery_failed'
--
-- The ADD is what scans: every existing row is re-checked against the new
-- predicate. It cannot fail here — the new list is a strict superset of the old
-- one, so any row that satisfied the old CHECK satisfies this one. (A migration
-- that NARROWED the list would be the dangerous shape, and would need the
-- pre-flight query §10 prescribes for the constraints that can fail.)
ALTER TABLE "orders" DROP CONSTRAINT "orders_status_check";--> statement-breakpoint
-- I9 — the set of states an order may be in, from Phase 3 onward.
--
-- This list bounds which values may EXIST. It does not make any of them
-- terminal: terminality is the status-guarded UPDATE, unchanged since 0000_init.
--
--   UPDATE orders SET status = $2, updated_at = now()
--   WHERE id = $1 AND status = ANY($3)   -- permitted source states only
--   RETURNING *;
--   -- 0 rows => the order was not in a state this transition may leave from;
--   --           the caller does nothing. A late webhook cannot resurrect a
--   --           completed order, and this new value changes none of that.
--
-- `delivery_failed` is RECOVERABLE, not terminal: it appears in a later
-- transition's `from` list (`retryIssuance`, slice 5) precisely so an operator
-- can push the order back into `delivering`. Adding it to I9's terminal set
-- instead would make that retry illegal by construction.
--
-- It is also the shop's fact, not the supplier's. "The supplier never answered"
-- is `issuance_attempts.status = 'unknown'` with `last_error` NULL, in a
-- different table, and a delivery recorded as failed must never overwrite it.
ALTER TABLE "orders" ADD CONSTRAINT "orders_status_check" CHECK ("status" IN ('created', 'paid', 'delivering', 'delivered', 'payment_failed', 'out_of_stock', 'delivery_failed'));
